  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Two families of adjectives",
     "texto": [
      "Japanese has two types of adjectives. The first ones end in **い** and are called -i adjectives: **おおきい** (big), **たかい** (expensive, tall), **あたらしい** (new).",
      "They go before the noun with nothing in between: **あたらしい くるま** (new car).",
      "At the end of the sentence **です** is added to be polite: **たかい です** (it's expensive)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Negating an -i adjective",
     "texto": [
      "These adjectives conjugate on their own. To negate, the final **い** changes to **く** and **ない** is added: **たかい** → **たかく ない です** (it's not expensive).",
      "A very common exception: **いい** (good) is negated as **よく ない** (not good).",
      "You don't say «たかい じゃ ありません»: that is only for nouns and the other type of adjectives."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "tabla",
     "titulo": "-i adjectives",
     "columnas": [
      {
